 \begin{abstract}
 \noindent
-Weather and climate are a recognised theme of the Victorian novel, but accounts of this theme typically rest on a few canonical texts. We ask how the vocabulary of weather (rain, wind, storm, fog, snow, sun, cloud and related words) varies across English-language novels by authors born in six cohorts, 1770--1799 to 1880--1899. Using 84 novels by 69 authors from Project Gutenberg (14 novels per cohort, 9.2 million tokens), we count a hand-built dictionary of 90 exact word forms per 10,000 tokens, compare cohorts with log-likelihood keyness and log ratios, and check the dictionary hits against a hand-judged sample of 198 passages. The pooled weather rate is stable across cohorts, between about 11 and 13 hits per 10,000 tokens, and cohort differences are small relative to the spread between authors. Beneath this stability, individual word families move: snow and ice words rise from 0.82 hits per 10,000 tokens in the first cohort to a peak of 2.06 in the fifth and 1.44 in the sixth, a pattern we regard as suggestive, and \emph{tempest} is concentrated in the earliest cohort. Fog and mist words are also more frequent in the sixth cohort, but precision of the dictionary is 0.71 overall and only 0.42 for storm words, 0.50 for fog words and 0.58 for cloud words, so these three families partly measure figurative language; the hand check found none of four sampled fog hits in the sixth cohort to be weather, and the fog rise cannot be read as more fog description. A reduced lexicon defined after the hand check makes the composite flat, weakens the fog rise and yields a steadier fall of storm words, which we treat as a pattern worth testing, not a finding. All patterns are descriptive and refer to this corpus of popular, digitised novels; none is a causal estimate.
+Weather and climate are a recognised theme of the Victorian novel, but accounts of this theme typically rest on a few canonical texts. We ask how the vocabulary of weather (rain, wind, storm, fog, snow, sun, cloud and related words) varies across English-language novels by authors born in six cohorts, 1770--1799 to 1880--1899. Using 84 novels by 69 authors from Project Gutenberg (14 novels per cohort, 9.2 million tokens), we count a hand-built dictionary of 90 exact word forms per 10,000 tokens, compare cohorts with log-likelihood keyness and log ratios, and check the dictionary hits against a hand-judged sample of 198 passages. The pooled weather rate is stable across cohorts, between about 11 and 13 hits per 10,000 tokens, and the range of author rates is much wider than the range of cohort rates, although the author-bootstrap intervals are wide enough to leave sizeable cohort differences open. Beneath this stability, individual word families move: snow and ice words rise from 0.82 hits per 10,000 tokens in the first cohort to a peak of 2.06 in the fifth and 1.44 in the sixth, a pattern we regard as suggestive, and \emph{tempest} is concentrated in the earliest cohort. Fog and mist words are also more frequent in the sixth cohort, but precision in the hand-checked sample (not weighted by term frequency) is 0.71 overall and only 0.42 for storm words, 0.50 for fog words and 0.58 for cloud words, so these three families partly measure figurative language; the hand check found none of only four sampled fog hits in the sixth cohort to be weather (too few to bound the share well), and the fog rise cannot be read as more fog description. A reduced lexicon defined after the hand check makes the composite flat, lowers the rank correlation of the fog rise while the fog and mist rate still roughly doubles, and yields a steadier fall of storm words; because this lexicon was fitted to the hand-checked sample, we treat the storm fall as a pattern worth testing, not a finding. All patterns are descriptive and refer to this corpus of popular, digitised novels; none is a causal estimate.
 
 \medskip
 \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} weather vocabulary; English-language novel; author cohorts; keyness; dictionary validation; Project Gutenberg; computational literary studies
